    <comment>Composed in the frame's model rather than fixed per control. {0} is the attempt under way and {1} how many are made before the client stops and asks.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Workspace.InfoBar.DeploymentLost.Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Your MailFathom is not answering</value>
    <comment>Heads the notice shown once the client has stopped asking on its own. Kept apart from a session that could not be had, because a connection is looked at and a sign-in is repeated.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Workspace.InfoBar.DeploymentLost.Message" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Nothing answered at your deployment, so the client stopped trying. Check that this device is online and that your deployment is running, then try again.</value>
    <comment>Names the two things the person can check. It says nothing about credentials, which would send somebody to their operator about a network.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Workspace.Toggle.Mailboxes.[using:Microsoft.UI.Xaml.Automation]AutomationProperties.Name" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Show or hide your mailboxes</value>
    <comment>Names the control that opens and closes the mailbox pane, which carries an icon rather than words.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Workspace.TextBlock.Mailboxes.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Your mailboxes</value>
    <comment>Heads the tree of the signed-in owner's own mailboxes. Never a deployment-wide list, which the client does not show.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Workspace.InfoBar.MailboxesPaused.Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>This deployment is not refreshing mail</value>
    <comment>Heads the notice shown where synchronization is switched off for the whole deployment, which changes what every sentence below it means.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Workspace.InfoBar.MailboxesPaused.Message" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Nothing below is being kept up to date, however recent it looks. Ask whoever runs your MailFathom to turn synchronization back on.</value>
    <comment>Says what the switch does to the readings beside it, and names the person who can change it.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Workspace.InfoBar.NoMailbox.Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>No mailbox yet</value>
    <comment>Heads the state an owner with no mail account is shown. It is a state rather than a failure, so it is not worded as one.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Workspace.InfoBar.NoMailbox.Message" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>There is no mail account on your MailFathom yet, so there is nothing to read here. Adding one is done by whoever runs it.</value>
    <comment>Says what to do about it rather than leaving an empty tree. Adding an account is an administrative act, which is deliberately not offered here.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Workspace.InfoBar.MailboxesUnavailable.Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Your mailboxes could not be read</value>
    <comment>Heads the failed read of the tree, which is a different thing from a mailbox being out of date.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Workspace.InfoBar.MailboxesUnavailable.Message" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Until your deployment answers, the tree is not shown. The notice above the workspace says what the client makes of the connection.</value>
    <comment>Sends the reader to the frame, which is where reaching the deployment at all is told apart from a mailbox being behind.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Workspace.Button.RetryMailboxes.Content" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Try again</value>
    <comment>Asks the deployment once more, which asks for the session and the mailboxes together.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Workspace.Button.OpenMailboxRow.[using:Microsoft.UI.Xaml.Automation]AutomationProperties.Name" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Show what is inside</value>
    <comment>Names the disclosure control on a row with something nested under it that is not being shown.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Workspace.Button.CloseMailboxRow.[using:Microsoft.UI.Xaml.Automation]AutomationProperties.Name" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Hide what is inside</value>
    <comment>Names the disclosure control on a row whose contents are being shown.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Workspace.TextBlock.MailboxBehind.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>mail still to take in</value>
    <comment>Said beside a standing rather than instead of one, because a copy can be behind under any of them. Lower case, since it follows the standing on the same line.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="WorkspaceScope.Role" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>{0} · every mailbox</value>
    <comment>One special-use folder taken across every mailbox. {0} is the role's own name in the reader's language; punctuation rather than words, so the name stays readable in any of them.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Mailboxes.Everything" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>All your mail</value>
    <comment>Names the row that scopes the workspace to every mailbox at once. Resolved in code rather than by a x:Uid, because the tree composes its rows from what the deployment answered.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Mailboxes.Unified" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>{0} · every mailbox</value>
    <comment>Names a role taken across every mailbox that plays it. {0} is the role's own name in the reader's language.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Mailboxes.Role.Inbox" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Inbox</value>
    <comment>The role a mail server advertises for incoming mail. Named here rather than taken from the folder's own name, which differs per provider and per language.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Mailboxes.Role.Drafts" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Drafts</value>
    <comment>The role a mail server advertises for unsent drafts.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Mailboxes.Role.Sent" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Sent</value>
    <comment>The role a mail server advertises for sent messages.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Mailboxes.Role.Outbox" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Outbox</value>
    <comment>The one role that is MailFathom's own rather than the mail server's: the folder an operator chose to see undelivered outgoing mail in.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Mailboxes.Role.Archive" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Archive</value>
    <comment>The role a mail server advertises for archived messages.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Mailboxes.Role.Junk" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Junk</value>
    <comment>The role a mail server advertises for messages classified as spam.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Mailboxes.Role.Trash" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Trash</value>
    <comment>The role a mail server advertises for deleted messages.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Mailboxes.Role.All" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>All mail</value>
    <comment>The virtual folder a mail server advertises as presenting every message in the account.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Mailboxes.Role.Flagged" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Flagged</value>
    <comment>The virtual folder a mail server advertises as presenting flagged messages.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Mailboxes.Role.Important" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Important</value>
    <comment>The virtual folder a mail server advertises as presenting messages it considers important.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Mailboxes.Standing.Unrecognized" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>State not recognized</value>
    <comment>Shown where the deployment named a state this version of the client does not know. Nothing is claimed about the copy, which is the safe reading.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Mailboxes.Standing.NeverSynchronized" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Not synchronized yet</value>
    <comment>Shown where nothing has ever been taken in.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Mailboxes.Standing.Synchronized" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Being refreshed</value>
    <comment>Shown where the deployment's last attempt did not fail. It says the copy is being kept current, and deliberately not that it is current — how old is too old is the reader's judgement.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Mailboxes.Standing.Failing" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Last refresh did not finish</value>
    <comment>Shown where the deployment's last attempt did not complete for a reason other than the mail server being out of reach. It is a mapping, a credential, or a defect rather than something to wait out.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Mailboxes.Standing.Unreachable" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Mail server not answering</value>
    <comment>Shown where the mail server did not serve the deployment within its budget, so nothing is refreshing the copy and nothing is wrong with it. Kept apart from the entry above because one is waited out and the other is repaired.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Mailboxes.Freshness.Never" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>No mail taken in yet</value>
    <comment>The gap where nothing has ever been synchronized and there is no instant to state.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Mailboxes.Freshness.WithinTheHour" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Updated within the last hour</value>
    <comment>The narrowest gap band. A timestamp slightly ahead of this device's clock reads as this band rather than as a negative gap.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Mailboxes.Freshness.Today" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Updated within the last day</value>
    <comment>The gap band for a copy refreshed less than a day ago.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Mailboxes.Freshness.WithinTheWeek" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Updated within the last week</value>
    <comment>The gap band for a copy refreshed less than a week ago.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Mailboxes.Freshness.LongerAgo" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Nothing taken in for over a week</value>
    <comment>The widest gap band. It states the gap without calling the copy stale, which is the reader's judgement rather than the client's.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Messages.NoSender" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Unknown sender</value>
    <comment>Stands in for a message whose sender no header carried, so the column reads as a message rather than as a row that failed to load.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Messages.NoSubject" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>(no subject)</value>
    <comment>Stands in for a message carrying no subject, on the same terms.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Messages.NoDate" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>No date</value>
    <comment>Stands in for a message no header dated, which the timeline still orders by what it recorded.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Messages.MoreRecipients" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>{0} +{1}</value>
    <comment>Names the first recipient beside how many others there are, for a row of mail this owner sent. {0} is that recipient and {1} is the count of the rest.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Messages.Announcement" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>{0}. {1}. {2}.</value>
    <comment>The whole row as one sentence for a screen reader: {0} is who the message is with, {1} is what it is about, and {2} is when it arrived.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Messages.Announcement.Unread" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Unread.</value>
    <comment>Appended to the sentence above where the row has not been read, since the mark that says so is drawn rather than announced.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Messages.Announcement.Flagged" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Flagged.</value>
    <comment>Appended on the same terms for a flagged row.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Messages.Announcement.Answered" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Answered.</value>
    <comment>Appended on the same terms for a row that has been answered.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="Messages.Announcement.Attachments" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Has attachments.</value>
    <comment>Appended on the same terms for a row carrying an attachment.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="MailBody.Block.Unsupported" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>This part of the message needs a newer version of MailFathom to be shown.</value>
    <comment>What stands where a block of the document is one this build does not implement. It names the absence rather than the block's own identity, which would be a name from a service the reader does not run.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="MailBody.Image.Undrawn" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>A picture that could not be shown</value>
    <comment>What a picture is described as where the message said nothing about it and nothing could be drawn from it.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="MailBody.Link.Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Follow this link?</value>
    <comment>The question asked before any link in a message is followed.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="MailBody.Link.DisplayText" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>The message shows</value>
    <comment>Labels the words the sender put on the link, which is the half a deceptive link controls.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="MailBody.Link.Target" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>It actually goes to</value>
    <comment>Labels the address the link opens, as the deployment resolved it.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="MailBody.Link.Punycode" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>The same address in ASCII</value>
    <comment>Labels the ASCII spelling of a host written in another script, shown only where the two spellings differ.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="MailBody.Link.Deception" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>The words on this link name a different site from the one it opens.</value>
    <comment>The warning shown where the deployment established that the link's own text names one host and the link goes to another.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="MailBody.Link.Homograph" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>This address is written in a script that can imitate another. Compare both spellings above before opening it.</value>
    <comment>The warning shown where the host has two spellings and the deployment found no mismatch between the link's text and its target.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="MailBody.Link.Unjudged" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>This version cannot read what the deployment said about this link, so nothing about it has been checked here.</value>
    <comment>The warning shown where the deployment reported a deception verdict this build does not recognize.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="MailBody.Link.Open" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Open in browser</value>
    <comment>The answer that follows the link, which hands it to the platform and leaves the application.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="MailBody.Link.Cancel" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Stay here</value>
    <comment>The answer that does not follow the link.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="MailBody.Refusal.NoHtmlPart" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>This message was written as plain text, so this is all of it.</value>
    <comment>Why a message is read as words. This one is not a shortcoming: the message carried nothing else.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="MailBody.Refusal.ReductionFailed" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>The formatted version of this message could not be read, so it is shown as text.</value>
    <comment>Why a message is read as words where the deployment could not reduce its formatted version.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="MailBody.Refusal.NothingRenderable" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>The formatted version of this message held nothing to show, so it is shown as text.</value>
    <comment>Why a message is read as words where its formatted version reduced to no content at all.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="MailBody.Refusal.Unrecognized" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>MailFathom gave a reason this version of the app does not know, so this message is shown as text.</value>
    <comment>Why a message is read as words where the deployment is ahead of this build. Said plainly rather than hidden: an unexplained fallback reads as a defect in the message.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="MailBody.Availability.EncryptedNotReadableLocally" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>This message arrived encrypted, so MailFathom cannot read it.</value>
    <comment>Why there is no body at all. The deployment states the state and the client names it; a state added there is a missing string here rather than a blank pane.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="MailBody.Availability.NotStoredExceededSizeLimit" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>This message is larger than this deployment stores, so its content was never kept.</value>
    <comment>Why there is no body at all, in the state a later refresh will not change.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="MailBody.Availability.NotStoredAwaitingStorageHeadroom" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>This deployment had no room for this message's content yet. It will be fetched on a later refresh.</value>
    <comment>Why there is no body at all, in the state that is temporary.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="MailBody.Availability.Unrecognized" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>This deployment reported a state this version of MailFathom does not know, so the message is not shown.</value>
    <comment>What a state named by a deployment ahead of this build is read as. It claims nothing about the message rather than reading as one that is fine.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="MailBody.Notice.RemoteContent.Message" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>References removed: {0}. Loading them would tell the sender's servers your network address and that you opened this message.</value>
    <comment>What was withheld and what loading it would reveal. {0} is a count; the wording avoids a plural form the count would have to agree with.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="MailBody.Notice.RemoteContentShown.Message" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Loaded from other servers: {0}. Those servers were told your network address and that this message was opened.</value>
    <comment>What this reading of the message did fetch, said plainly rather than left to be inferred. {0} is a count.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="MailBody.Notice.UndrawnImages.Message" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Pictures left out because they were too large or too many: {0}.</value>
    <comment>What of the message's own pictures was left undrawn. {0} is a count.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="MailBody.InfoBar.RemoteContent.Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Remote content blocked</value>
    <comment>Heads the notice offering the reader the choice to load what the message asks for from somebody else's server.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="MailBody.Button.ShowRemoteContent.Content" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Load once</value>
    <comment>The whole of the override: it re-reads this message with the references followed, and nothing writes the answer down.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="MailBody.InfoBar.RemoteContentShown.Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Remote content loaded</value>
    <comment>Heads the notice stating that this reading of the message fetched from somebody else's server.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="MailBody.InfoBar.PlainText.Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Shown as text</value>
    <comment>Heads the reason a message is read as words rather than drawn.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="MailBody.InfoBar.Truncated.Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Shortened</value>
    <comment>Heads the notice that a bound stopped the rendering before the end of the message.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="MailBody.InfoBar.Truncated.Message" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>This message is longer than what is shown here.</value>
    <comment>States that a bound stopped the rendering, without claiming which one did.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="MailBody.InfoBar.UndrawnImages.Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Some pictures were left out</value>
    <comment>Heads the notice counting the message's own pictures that were left undrawn.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="MailBody.InfoBar.DeploymentAhead.Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Newer than this version</value>
    <comment>Heads the notice that the deployment writes a revision of the body contract this build does not read.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="MailBody.InfoBar.DeploymentAhead.Message" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Your MailFathom deployment describes messages in a newer way than this application reads. Parts of this message may be missing.</value>
    <comment>Says what a newer deployment costs the reader, which is parts of a message rather than the message.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="MailBody.Empty.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Choose a message to read it here.</value>
    <comment>What the reading pane is before anything is opened in it.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="MailBody.Document.[using:Microsoft.UI.Xaml.Automation]AutomationProperties.Name" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Message body</value>
    <comment>What a screen reader announces the drawn document as.</comment>
  </data>
  <data name="MailBody.Words.[using:Microsoft.UI.Xaml.Automation]AutomationProperties.Name" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Message body as text</value>
    <comment>What a screen reader announces the message as where it is read as words rather than drawn.</comment>
  </data>
</root>
